\description{
Quantify uncertainty in fixed non-Person facet locations, two-way interaction
cells, or prespecified linear contrasts from a supported \code{\link[=fit_mfrm]{fit_mfrm()}} result.
Inspect \code{Estimate}, \code{Status}, \code{CIEligible}, \code{CoverageQualified}, and
\code{InferenceReview} together in \code{summary(intervals)}: missing bounds do not
imply zero uncertainty, and \code{Status = "fixed"} marks constraint-pinned
targets without inferential intervals.
}
\details{
\strong{Covariance propagation and constraints.} For constrained locations
\eqn{b = b_0 + J\eta} and contrast matrix \eqn{C}, target covariance is
\eqn{C J V_\eta J^\top C^\top}, where \eqn{V_\eta} is extracted after
inverting the \strong{full joint} information across all free structural, step,
interaction, slope, and population coordinates (\code{Status = "information_unavailable"} if singular; \code{Status = "insufficient_cluster_rank"}
withholds sandwich bounds while retaining \verb{Model*} columns).

\strong{Standardized contrasts.} With \code{scale = "standardized"}, native contrast
\eqn{d} and log-variance \eqn{\lambda = \log(\sigma^2)} define \eqn{t =
d/\sigma} with full delta-method variance:
\deqn{\widehat{\mathrm{Var}}(\widehat t) =
  \frac{\widehat{\mathrm{Var}}(\widehat d)
    + \widehat d^2\widehat{\mathrm{Var}}(\widehat\lambda)/4
    - \widehat d\,\widehat{\mathrm{Cov}}(\widehat d,\widehat\lambda)}
    {\widehat\sigma^2}.}

\strong{Anchor-source propagation.} With \code{anchor_covariance} and
\code{independent_anchors = TRUE}, implicit differentiation \eqn{S =
-H_{uu}^{-1}H_{ua}} yields source derivative \eqn{D = t_u S + t_a} and total
covariance \eqn{t_u V_{\mathrm{cond}} t_u^\top + D V_{\mathrm{anchor}}
D^\top}, retaining conditional bounds in \verb{Conditional*} and reusable by
\code{\link[=mfrm_population_intervals]{mfrm_population_intervals()}}.

\strong{CML and profile contrasts.} Full CML uses unregularized inverse
conditional information (\code{scale = "native"}); comparison-only CML reuses
saved subspace covariances without refitting. Profile contrasts report
\code{ProfileEstimate} alongside \code{SourceEstimate} without Wald \code{SE}/\code{vcov()}.
Worked examples and bounded coverage evaluations appear on the package
website
(\url{https://ryuya-dot-com.github.io/mfrmr/articles/mfrmr-facet-intervals.html}).

\code{plot()} method: Plot fixed-facet interval methods

For \code{plot()}: Fixed targets use open diamonds without inferential intervals.
A conditionally fixed anchor can have a source-propagated interval; the
comparison displays its conditional fixed value separately. Method labels
distinguish source propagation from conditional observed information.
Unavailable intervals in either displayed method use crosses; an ordinary comparison interval
does not substitute for a missing sandwich interval. The zero line is a
reference, not a threshold of practical importance or a rater-quality rule.
No fit or covariance is recalculated. Target order is preserved.
Weak-information cautions remain in the returned data and appear in the
default subtitle. Custom or omitted subtitles change display only.
For experimental two-family GPCM locations, the axis uses fixed-N(0,1)
ability units and the default subtitle states the location reference and
the lack of established coverage, with a deficient Person-score rank caution when
applicable. These are not uniform expected-rating differences.
Standardized contrast axes use population SD units; returned notes identify
whether SD uncertainty is estimated or the known SD is one. Reviewed
interaction contrasts retain group direction, panel weights and scale in
their source result; returned labels distinguish them from facet locations.
Native CML intervals are labeled conditional information. Their returned
notes describe asymptotic validity without implying weak information or
an estimated ability population SD.
When a comparison has no estimate, its row is labeled "not estimated"
and has no point or interval. An empty display range is only an axis
placeholder; it does not assign a value to unavailable targets.
Joint interaction-cell results retain cell identities, counts and sparse
flags in \code{plot_data(p, "interaction_cells")}. Their axis uses additive
predictor shifts; row labels show the two level names in fitted facet order.
Units follow the fixed N(0,1) ability scale. Positive effects raise
expected coded scores relative to the jointly fitted main effects. These
pointwise intervals are not fixed-baseline bias screens or fairness labels.
}
